"63_winui_controls_tests.mst — WinUiControlsTests, the headless half of WG3's
 acceptance gate (docs/sprints/tests_wg3.md, 'In-language tests'). Exercises the
 guest side of the flag-and-drain pass (world/91_winui_shell.mst) and of the
 layout object (world/92_winui_controls.mst).

 WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE, and the line is the same one 61 and 62 drew:
 anything that needs a WINDOW, a message PUMP, a modal loop or a real control.
 Coalescing, tracking suppression, a synthesised click and a WM_NOTIFY carrying
 a live NMHDR are all claims about what happens when WINDOWS calls the
 trampoline, and Windows will not call a trampoline in a `cargo test` worker
 thread with no window and no loop. Those live in `just gate-wg3`, driven
 through MACVM_WINUI_CTL against one process with one thread and one real
 window, and in the door's own Rust unit tests (src/runtime/win_wndproc.rs).

 WHAT REMAINS is the half worth having in-language, and WG3's split was designed
 so that it is a big half:

   * `WinLayout` is PURE. A client size and a DPI in, a Dictionary of rects
     out — no handle, no Win32, no window, and no winkb either. Its two tests
     therefore run on every machine this suite runs on, including a Mac.
   * `drainPass` is ORDINARY SMALLTALK. It can be SENT. Flags set, pass run,
     flags clear, work counted once — the whole contract, as a method rather
     than as a system.

 THREE SKIP LEVELS, all announced:
   * NOT WINDOWS — the runner never calls us (99_run_all.mst's guard).
   * NO DATABASE — the parts that name a Win32 message ask winkb for its
     number, so without windows_api.db they cannot run. The layout tests need
     neither winkb nor Win32 and always do.

 LOADING: as 62 — `WinShell`, `WinLayout` and `WinControl` live in the
 conditional winui layer, so world/tests/tests.list names ../90, ../91 and ../92
 above this file (WG0's Δ 6: an unresolved capitalised name in a method body is
 a COMPILE error in this dialect, so a layered world's test file cannot guard
 itself at run time)."

TestCase subclass: WinUiControlsTests [

    runAll [
        "The layout is pure arithmetic and runs whatever else is true of this
         machine — no database, no Win32, no window. It is also the piece D4
         is actually about, so it goes first."
        self runTest: #testLayoutComputesRects do: [ self testLayoutComputesRects ].
        self runTest: #testLayoutScalesWithDpi do: [ self testLayoutScalesWithDpi ].
        self runTest: #testLayoutRefusesTwoFillBands do: [ self testLayoutRefusesTwoFillBands ].
        self runTest: #testControlWithoutWindowFailsCleanly
             do: [ self testControlWithoutWindowFailsCleanly ].

        WinApi winkbAvailable ifFalse: [
            Transcript showCr:
                '  SKIP WinUiControlsTests (5 of 9) — windows_api.db absent on this machine.'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       The drain records messages by their winkb-queried numbers (it'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       transcribes none), so testDrainClearsFlags, testDrainCoalesces,'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       testDrainReaskIsOneShot, testNotifyOffsetsMatchWinkb and'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       testWg3MessagesMatchWinkb cannot run. The database is a'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       machine-local artifact, not a build input; nothing here is broken.'.
            ^self ].

        self runTest: #testDrainClearsFlags do: [ self testDrainClearsFlags ].
        self runTest: #testDrainCoalesces do: [ self testDrainCoalesces ].
        self runTest: #testDrainReaskIsOneShot do: [ self testDrainReaskIsOneShot ].
        self runTest: #testNotifyOffsetsMatchWinkb do: [ self testNotifyOffsetsMatchWinkb ].
        self runTest: #testWg3MessagesMatchWinkb do: [ self testWg3MessagesMatchWinkb ].
    ]

    "── D4: the layout object, pure ──────────────────────────────────────"

    testLayoutComputesRects [
        "A known client size in, known rects out. The numbers are written here
         rather than derived, on purpose: a test that recomputed the layout
         with the layout's own arithmetic would agree with any bug it had.

         The shell layout is three bands with a 10-DIP margin — a command band
         32 tall, a status band 26, and a list filling the rest — so at 900x600
         and 96 dpi: x is the margin, width is the client width less two
         margins, and each y is the previous y plus that band's height plus a
         margin. The filler's height is what is left, and 600 - 4*10 - 32 - 26
         is 502."
        | r |
        r := WinLayout shell rectsIn: 900 by: 600 dpi: 96.
        self assert: (r at: #button) equals: #(10 10 880 32).
        self assert: (r at: #status) equals: #(10 52 880 26).
        self assert: (r at: #list) equals: #(10 88 880 502).
        "Every band is inside the client area, which is the property a wrong
         sign or a wrong subtraction would break while still producing four
         plausible integers."
        r keysAndValuesDo: [ :k :v |
            self assert: (v at: 1) >= 0.
            self assert: (v at: 2) >= 0.
            self assert: ((v at: 1) + (v at: 3)) <= 900.
            self assert: ((v at: 2) + (v at: 4)) <= 600 ].
        "A window too small for its fixed bands clamps the filler at zero
         rather than producing a negative height — SetWindowPos would take a
         negative height and draw nothing, with no error anywhere."
        self assert: (((WinLayout shell rectsIn: 200 by: 40 dpi: 96) at: #list) at: 4) equals: 0
    ]

    testLayoutScalesWithDpi [
        "`tests_wg3.md`'s DPI row, and the reason `WinLayout` computes in DIP
         space and scales once at the end rather than multiplying as it goes:
         the same layout at 144 dpi must be EXACTLY 1.5x the layout at 96, not
         1.5x plus a rounding error per band. WG1 established the environment's
         DPI contract as an equality; this keeps it one."
        | a b |
        a := WinLayout shell rectsIn: 900 by: 600 dpi: 96.
        b := WinLayout shell rectsIn: 1350 by: 900 dpi: 144.
        a keysAndValuesDo: [ :k :v |
            1 to: 4 do: [ :i |
                self assert: ((b at: k) at: i) equals: (v at: i) * 3 // 2 ] ].
        "...and at 96 the DIP and the pixel are the same number, which is what
         `USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI` means and what makes the assertions above
         readable."
        self assert: ((a at: #button) at: 4) equals: 32
    ]

    testLayoutRefusesTwoFillBands [
        "A layout object that accepted an arrangement it cannot compute would
         answer four plausible integers for a band whose height nobody defined.
         Refusing is cheaper than debugging that."
        self assert: ((self errorFor: [ WinLayout new fill: #a; fill: #b ])
             includesSubstring: 'only one fill band')
    ]

    "── D3: a control without a window ───────────────────────────────────"

    testControlWithoutWindowFailsCleanly [
        "`tests_wg3.md`'s fourth in-language row. Creating a control before
         `openMain` must be a clean guest error NAMING the call — never
         CreateWindowExW against a NULL parent, which answers NULL and leaves
         a caller with no window, no control and no explanation.

         Needs no database and no Win32: the check is above both."
        | msg |
        msg := self errorFor: [
            WinControl name: #ghost class: 'BUTTON' text: 'x'
                       style: 0 exStyle: 0 id: 1 in: 0 ].
        self assert: (msg includesSubstring: 'ghost').
        self assert: (msg includesSubstring: 'openMain')
    ]

    "── D1: the drain pass, as a method ──────────────────────────────────"

    testDrainClearsFlags [
        "The pass's whole contract: flags set, pass run, flags clear, work
         recorded ONCE. Sent directly — no window, no pump, no trampoline —
         because `drainPass` is ordinary Smalltalk and the interesting property
         is not where it is called from but what it does with the flags.

         The layout arm is exercised with no window open, which is a real state
         (the drain outlives a close) and must be a no-op rather than a raise."
        | passes |
        WinShell resetDoorCounters.
        passes := WinShell drainPasses.
        self deny: WinShell pendingLayout.

        "A WM_SIZE through the dispatcher sets the flag and NOTHING else — the
         door is shallow, which is the entire architecture."
        WinShell window: 0 message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_SIZE')
                 wParam: 0 lParam: 16r02580190.
        self assert: WinShell pendingLayout.
        self assert: WinShell layoutCount equals: 0.
        self assert: WinShell sizeCount equals: 1.

        "The pass does the work and clears the flag."
        self assert: WinShell drainPass equals: 0.
        self assert: WinShell drainPasses equals: passes + 1.
        self assert: WinShell layoutCount equals: 1.
        self deny: WinShell pendingLayout.

        "And a second pass with nothing pending does NOT repeat the work. That
         is what 'clears them' has to mean, and a pass that re-laid-out on
         every heartbeat would pass every other assertion in this file."
        WinShell drainPass.
        self assert: WinShell layoutCount equals: 1
    ]

    testDrainCoalesces [
        "The sprint's central claim, in the one form that can be checked
         without a message loop: TEN messages, ONE pass, ONE unit of work.

         The real ratio is measured in `just gate-wg3` against a live pump and
         reported rather than asserted (tests_wg3.md item 3 says so — a ratio
         of 1.0 would mean the drain is not coalescing and the claim is false).
         This is its in-language twin, and it is the half that says WHY: the
         flag is a boolean, so the tenth message costs exactly what the second
         one did."
        WinShell resetDoorCounters.
        1 to: 10 do: [ :i |
            WinShell window: 0 message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_SIZE')
                     wParam: 0 lParam: (i * 65536) + 400 ].
        self assert: WinShell sizeCount equals: 10.
        self assert: WinShell layoutCount equals: 0.
        WinShell drainPass.
        self assert: WinShell layoutCount equals: 1.
        self assert: WinShell drainPasses < WinShell sizeCount.

        "Commands coalesce differently and deliberately so: a click is an EVENT
         and losing one would be a bug, so they QUEUE and the pass services
         every one — while a resize is a STATE and only the last matters. Both
         are 'coalescing'; only one of them may drop anything."
        WinShell resetDoorCounters.
        1 to: 5 do: [ :i |
            WinShell window: 0 message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_COMMAND')
                     wParam: 100 lParam: 0 ].
        self assert: WinShell pendingCommandCount equals: 5.
        WinShell drainPass.
        self assert: WinShell drainWorkCount equals: 5.
        self assert: WinShell pendingCommandCount equals: 0
    ]

    testDrainReaskIsOneShot [
        "Work discovered DURING a pass. The pass may not post its own wake —
         that is how a drain becomes a spin — so it answers non-zero instead
         and the heartbeat picks it up. Bounded, not exact: the property is
         that it stops."
        WinShell resetDoorCounters.
        WinShell askForAnotherPass.
        self assert: WinShell drainPass equals: 1.
        self assert: WinShell reaskCount equals: 1.
        "The next pass answers 'nothing more' — one-shot, so a handler that
         always asked again could not wedge the heartbeat."
        self assert: WinShell drainPass equals: 0.
        self assert: WinShell reaskCount equals: 1
    ]

    "── the two compensating controls for what Rust TRANSCRIBES ──────────"

    testNotifyOffsetsMatchWinkb [
        "WG3's own version of WG2's `testAllowlistMatchesWinkb`, and it exists
         for exactly the same reason.

         `runtime::win_wndproc::read_nmhdr` reads WM_NOTIFY's payload at
         hard-coded byte offsets 0, 8 and 16, because a Rust pointer read
         cannot query SQLite and the core crate must build on a machine with no
         windows_api.db at all. That is the thing D2 forbids everywhere else,
         so here is the compensating control: the SAME three offsets, asked of
         winkb by name. A wrong one would read a plausible wrong word — a
         control id that is really half a pointer — and nothing else would say
         so.

         The struct's SIZE is checked too, from a different database column, so
         'the offsets agree with each other' and 'the offsets are right' stay
         different claims."
        self assert: (WinApi offsetOf: 'NMHDR' field: 'hwndFrom') equals: 0.
        self assert: (WinApi offsetOf: 'NMHDR' field: 'idFrom') equals: 8.
        self assert: (WinApi offsetOf: 'NMHDR' field: 'code') equals: 16.
        self assert: (WinApi sizeOf: 'NMHDR') equals: 24
    ]

    testWg3MessagesMatchWinkb [
        "The five numbers WG3 added to the Rust allowlist, plus the two the
         trampoline handles itself. Same rule, same reason, same shape of
         check as WG2's."
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_NOTIFY') equals: 16r004E.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_ENTERSIZEMOVE') equals: 16r0231.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_EXITSIZEMOVE') equals: 16r0232.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_ENTERMENULOOP') equals: 16r0211.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_EXITMENULOOP') equals: 16r0212.
        "The drain's own plumbing: the heartbeat's message, and the base the
         private wake is offset from. `WM_APP_DRAIN` is WM_APP+7 in Rust, and
         the only thing the guest needs to be sure of is that WM_APP is where
         Windows says the application's own numbers start."
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_TIMER') equals: 16r0113.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_APP') equals: 16r8000.
        "The notification code the gate's list view produces, in the unsigned
         form both winkb and NMHDR.code carry it in — LVN_ITEMCHANGED is
         `(UINT)-101`, and comparing it against a NEGATIVE 101 anywhere in this
         layer would silently never match."
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'LVN_ITEMCHANGED') equals: 4294967195.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'NM_CUSTOMDRAW') equals: 4294967284
    ]

    errorFor: aBlock [
        ^[ aBlock value. '<<no error>>' ] on: Error do: [ :e | e messageText ]
    ]
]
